# name: test/sql/bugs/issue_220_variadic_parameters.test
# description: #220 — variadic parameters are extracted in go and cpp (dispatch was dead)
# group: [bugs]

# The derivation gate (#259) found the #220 shape alive in three more languages:
# go, cpp and swift each had `else if (strcmp(child_type, "variadic_parameter"))`
# calling a dedicated extractor, and "variadic_parameter" is in NONE of their
# symbol tables. The branch could never be taken, so variadic parameters were
# silently dropped -- no compile error, no warning, no failing test.
#
# Measured before the fix, on this fixture:
#     Sum(nums ...int)                      -> 0 parameters
#     Printf(format string, args ...T)      -> 1 parameter   (args missing)
#     pack(Args... args)                    -> name "...", type "variadic"
#
# NOT asserted here: `is_variadic` itself. `parameters` is exposed to SQL as
# STRUCT(name, type)[] only -- 2 of ParameterInfo's 6 fields, see #255 -- so the
# flag is invisible from a query. These assertions pin the half that IS
# observable: that the parameter appears at all, with its real name and type.

require sitting_duck

# =============================================================================
# go — the dispatch named a node the grammar does not emit
# =============================================================================
# The real node is `variadic_parameter_declaration`. Sum's parameter was absent
# entirely before the fix.

query III
SELECT name, len(parameters), list_transform(parameters, lambda p: p.name || ':' || p.type)::VARCHAR
FROM read_ast('test/data/variadic/sample.go', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declaration' ORDER BY name;
----
Plain	2	['a:int', 'b:string']
Printf	2	['format:string', 'args:...interface{}']
Sum	1	['nums:...int']

# `args:...interface{}` is the case that the node-name fix ALONE would not have
# caught: the extractor matched only `type_identifier` and `primitive_type`
# (the latter also absent from the go grammar), so an interface-typed variadic
# would have lost its type and then been dropped by the caller's
# `!param.type.empty()` guard. The type is now taken structurally -- whatever
# follows the `...` token.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: p.type)[2]
FROM read_ast('test/data/variadic/sample.go', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declaration' AND name = 'Printf';
----
...interface{}

# =============================================================================
# cpp — dispatch was dead AND the handler was a placeholder
# =============================================================================
# Before: name "...", type "variadic" -- constants, not the real identifiers,
# and unreachable anyway. `Args... args` parses as
#   variadic_parameter_declaration( type_identifier(Args), variadic_declarator(... args) )

query III
SELECT name, len(parameters), list_transform(parameters, lambda p: p.name || ':' || p.type)::VARCHAR
FROM read_ast('test/data/variadic/sample.cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition' ORDER BY name;
----
pack	1	['args:Args...']
plain	2	['a:int', 'b:double']

# =============================================================================
# swift — there is no variadic node type at all
# =============================================================================
# `func sum(_ numbers: Int...)` parses as an ordinary `parameter` whose children
# are simple_identifier simple_identifier ":" user_type "...", so the ellipsis is
# a plain token. The old branch dispatched on a node that does not exist in the
# grammar; it and its extractor are removed, and the token is detected instead.
#
# Only the non-regression is assertable here -- the flag it now sets is not
# exposed to SQL (#255). What this pins is that removing the dead branch did not
# disturb ordinary swift parameter extraction.

query III
SELECT name, len(parameters), list_transform(parameters, lambda p: p.name || ':' || p.type)::VARCHAR
FROM read_ast('test/data/variadic/sample.swift', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declaration' ORDER BY name;
----
plain	2	['a:Int', 'b:String']
sum	1	['_ numbers:Int']
